Please review the current code and lay out a plan to refactor things according to the below critique.

Your SyncService.java uses a Timer that runs every 20 seconds and spawns six separate threads for different sync tasks.

Java

Timer timer = new Timer();
timer.schedule(new TimerTask() {
 @Override
 public void run() {
 // ...
 Thread threadDownloadTrips = new Thread(...);
 Thread threadCompletedTrip = new Thread(...);
 // ... 4 more threads
 threadDownloadTrips.start();
 // ...
 }
}, 0, 20000);
Issues:

Battery Drain: This constant 20-second polling interval will significantly drain the device's battery, as the CPU and network hardware can never properly sleep.

Redundant Work: The app downloads the entire list of files from Dropbox every 20 seconds (listFolder), even if nothing has changed. This is inefficient in terms of data usage and API calls.

Race Conditions: You are creating and destroying threads and database connections very frequently. While you use .join() to wait for them, this is an unconventional and potentially fragile way to manage background tasks. For example, multiple threads are calling openDatabase(), which could lead to contention issues.

Recommendations:

Use WorkManager: Android's WorkManager is the modern, recommended solution for deferrable and guaranteed background work. It is battery-aware and can schedule tasks to run under optimal conditions (e.g., when the device is charging or connected to Wi-Fi). You could schedule a periodic Worker to run every 15 minutes (the minimum allowed) instead of every 20 seconds.

Implement Exponential Backoff: If a sync fails (e.g., no network), you should not retry again in just 20 seconds. Implement an exponential backoff strategy (e.g., wait 1 min, then 2 mins, then 4 mins) to save battery and avoid spamming the server. WorkManager has this built-in.

Use a More Efficient Dropbox API: Instead of listing all files every time, you can use the listFolderContinue endpoint from the Dropbox API. This allows you to get a cursor on the first call and then only ask for changes since the last sync, which is much more efficient.
</user_query>"}]},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user wants me to review the current code and create a plan to refactor it according to the critique about SyncService.java. The critique mentions several issues:

